1,1,8,8,8,0,0,8,8,8,4,4,8,8],[8,1,1,1,1,8,0,0,0,0,8,4,4,4,4,8],[8,1,1,1,1,8,0,0,0,0,8,4,4,4,4,8],[8,8,1,1,8,8,8,0,0,8,8,8,4,4,8,8],[0,8,8,8,8,0,8,8,8,8,0,8,8,8,8,0],[8,8,4,4,8,8,8,4,4,8,8,8,4,4,8,8],[8,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,8],[8,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,8,4,4,4,4,8],[8,8,4,4,8,8,8,4,4,8,8,8,4,4,8,8],[0,8,8,8,8,0,8,8,8,8,0,8,8,8,8,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-fable-5-1"],"explanation":"The check confirms the rule exactly on both train pairs, and the test behaviour (117 and 104 changed cells) is exactly 13 cells per marked square, which is what I expect. Here is the worked solution.\n\n## What the grid is made of\n\nEvery input is a lattice of 8-coloured lines spaced 5 apart. The lines cross at lattice points (0,0), (0,5), (0,10), … and inside each 4×4 cell between the lines sits a \"rounded square\" of 12 black (0) cells — a 4×4 block with the four corners cut off. In an undisturbed grid every lattice point is 0.\n\nA few lattice points carry a different colour instead of 0: in pair 1 only (10,0)=4, plus (5,10)=8 (background colour, which also counts as \"not 0\"); in pair 2, (0,0)=7, (5,10)=1, (10,5)=4. Those are the markers.\n\n## What changes\n\nThe fact sheet's change list is very regular: for each marker, 12 cells turn into the marker's colour and 1 cell (the marker itself) turns to 0. Reading off which 12 cells:\n\n- Pair 2, marker 7 at (0,0) → the rounded square with bbox [1,1,4,4] becomes 7. That square sits immediately down-right of the marker.\n- Marker 1 at (5,10) → square [6,11,9,14] becomes 1 — again directly down-right.\n- Marker 4 at (10,5) → square [11,6,14,9] becomes 4.\n- Pair 1, marker 4 at (10,0) → square [11,1,14,4] becomes 4; marker 8 at (5,10) → square [6,11,9,14] is filled with 8, i.e. erased into the background.\n\nSo the rule is: **each marker sitting at a lattice point is \"poured\" into the rounded square whose bounding-box corner is diagonally down-right of it. The square takes the marker's colour, and the lattice point is restored to 0.** Markers of colour 8 are treated the same way, which erases that square — pair 1 demonstrates this. My inspect run implemented exactly this and reproduced both train outputs with zero mismatches.\n\nThe square is found by its own geometry, not by absolute position: for each black object of 12 cells with box (y0,x0,y1,x1), the marker position is (y0−1, x0−1). That makes the rule indifferent to grid size, which matters because test 1 is 21 rows tall.\n\n## Applying it to the test inputs\n\n**Test 1 (21×16):** the lattice points in rows 0, 5 and 10 are mostly 8 (background) — only (5,5) is 1 — while row 15's lattice points are 0. So the three squares in rows 1–4 are erased to 8, in rows 6–9 the centre square becomes 1 and the outer two are erased, in rows 11–14 all three are erased, and the bottom band (rows 16–19) stays black since its markers are already 0. All used lattice points are set to 0.\n\n**Test 2 (16×16):** markers 1 at (0,0),(0,5),(0,10),(5,0) → the whole top row of squares and the middle-left square become 1; markers 4 at (5,10),(10,0),(10,5),(10,10) → the middle-right square and the whole bottom row become 4; (5,5) is already 0 so the centre square stays black.\n\nThe program emits one stage per filled square so each edit is visible.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    emit(g, \"Start: a lattice of 8 lines with black rounded squares; some lattice points carry a marker colour.\")\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg):\n        if o[\"colour\"] != 0 or o[\"size\"] < 2:\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        my, mx = y0 - 1, x0 - 1\n        if my < 0 or mx < 0:\n            continue\n        c = g[my, mx]\n        if c == 0:\n            continue\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            g[y, x] = c\n        g[my, mx] = 0\n        emit(g, f\"Marker colour {c} at lattice point ({my},{mx}) pours into the square just down-right of it (rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1}); the lattice point is reset to 0.\")\n    emit(g, \"All markers have been poured into their squares.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The square takes the marker's colour, and the lattice point is restored to 0.** Markers of colour 8 are treated the same way, which erases that square — pair 1 demonstrates this."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: **each marker sitting at a lattice point is \"poured\" into the rounded square whose bounding-box corner is diagonally down-right of it."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Marker colour 8 at lattice point (5,10) pours into the square just down-right of it (rows 6-9, cols 11-14); the lattice point is reset to 0."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Marker colour 4 at lattice point (10,0) pours into the square just down-right of it (rows 11-14, cols 1-4); the lattice point is reset to 0."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ifferent colour instead of 0: in pair 1 only (10,0)=4, plus (5,10)=8 (background colour, which also counts as \"not 0\"); in pair 2, (0,0)=7, (5,10)=1, (10,5)=4."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"3845f5b747b74ea61f440986c7ced2c006f9ba62a7ab2694e0e1b155aedfa0c7","task_sha256":"6382e6501ae19791e4a97207fc1a690246635f7509a98fc6373ce3cac5e03d45","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
